(* Variant quic: the session handshake over QUIC, as the code has it (AUD-5, #389). msquic gives no TLS
   exporter, so the binding is the tls-server-end-point of RFC 5929, the hash of the certificate that the
   server presents (QuicCertBinding, NOISE_BINDING_SERVER_END_POINT). It is the same for every connection to
   that certificate, which the model shows: an attacker that holds the key of the certificate of bob, and
   nothing else, relays the handshake of alice to bob over a TLS session of its own. The client pins the
   certificate (QuicConnCallback). The keys are leaked as in tls443. *)

process
  ( Setup()
  | Leaks()
  | !( Client(alice, bob, pin_on, bt_endpoint, plabel_session, lab_exp_session, suite_hfs) )
  | !( new sid: sessionid; Server(bob, alice, bt_endpoint, plabel_session, lab_exp_session, suite_ik, suite_hfs, sid) )
  )
